# Thread sanitizer suppressions for the linux-tsan preset (docs/build.md, "Presets").
#
# Only third-party code belongs here, and only where the sanitizer cannot see what orders it. A
# race in Sonnet's own code is a bug to fix, never one to suppress.

# Jolt, all of it. vcpkg ships a prebuilt libJolt.a with no sanitizer instrumentation, so the
# happens-before that Jolt establishes is invisible to the sanitizer wherever it is built inside
# the library rather than in a header. Two examples seen here:
#
#  - TempAllocatorImpl has a plain mTop and is written from several threads by design. Jolt's own
#    header says so: "allocations and frees can take place from different threads, but the order is
#    guaranteed though job dependencies, so it is not needed to use any form of locking". Those job
#    dependencies resolve in JobSystemWithBarrier.cpp, inside the uninstrumented library.
#  - Mutex, in builds defining JPH_ENABLE_ASSERTS, reads mLockedThreadID in try_lock() before it
#    holds the lock, to assert that lock and unlock share a thread. That one is a real race on a
#    value nothing but the assertion reads, and it is compiled out of builds without asserts.
#
# What this costs: a report whose stack passes through a JPH frame is suppressed, so the sanitizer
# does not police how `physics` drives Jolt either. Two things cover that instead — Jolt's Debug
# assertions, which check the temp allocator is freed in LIFO order and that a mutex is unlocked by
# the thread that locked it, and would fire loudly if the job ordering this adapter relies on were
# broken; and physics_tests, whose stress case settles a thousand-body pile identically with the
# pool idle and with it working.
#
# Lifting this needs an overlay port that builds Jolt with -fsanitize=thread for this preset alone,
# which is the only way to give the sanitizer the whole picture. Deferred, and noted in M8.
race:JPH::

# There is no Lavapipe entry here on purpose. Mesa's software rasterizer runs the Vulkan tests and
# starts a rasterizing pool of its own, uninstrumented like Jolt, and it reports races and
# lock-order inversions from inside its own threads by the hundred. Suppressing those needs both
# race: and deadlock: entries broad enough to cover engine frames, since the stacks enter through
# VulkanDevice, which would blind the sanitizer to the engine's own use of the device.
#
# The preset points VK_DRIVER_FILES at /dev/null instead, so every suite runs with no Vulkan driver
# and its GPU cases skip themselves: renderer_tests still runs 29 of its 38 on the null device,
# which is where the per-frame fill and everything else the job system touches is exercised. What
# this gives up is the sanitizer's view of the engine's Vulkan calls, and those are single-threaded
# by design (docs/rendering.md, "rhi per-frame rules"); ctest --preset linux-debug is what covers
# them, on Lavapipe, where the driver's own threads are not the sanitizer's problem.
